{
  "title": "OpenMRS Core \u2014 healthcare (1,836\u2605)",
  "content": "Project: OpenMRS Core\nDomain: healthcare | Scale: large | Stars: 1,836\nProblem: OpenMRS provides a free, customizable electronic medical record (EMR) system to improve healthcare delivery in resource-constrained environments. It offers a robust, scalable, open-source platform for managing patient-based medical records.\nTarget users: Healthcare providers, Hospitals and clinics, Resource-constrained healthcare settings, Medical institutions in developing regions, Healthcare system administrators\nCore features: Patient-based electronic medical records management; Customizable EMR system with modular architecture; RESTful API for third-party integrations; Multi-language and localization support; Module extension system for custom functionality; Web-based user interface for clinical workflows; Docker containerization support for deployment\nIntegrations: Maven repositories, Travis CI for continuous integration, Snyk for security scanning, Codacy for code quality analysis, Coveralls for coverage tracking\nTech stack: JavaScript with JSP/HTML for web interface + Java with Spring Framework + RDBMS (MySQL/PostgreSQL supported) + Apache Tomcat, Jetty, Docker, Maven\nKey libraries: Spring Framework, Hibernate ORM, Maven Build Tool, JUnit for testing\nArchitecture: monolith\nDesign lessons:\n- Modular design with plugin-based extensions enables customization without core modification, reducing maintenance burden for diverse deployment scenarios\n- Heavy emphasis on testing infrastructure (Snyk, Codacy, Coveralls, Travis CI) reflects mission-critical healthcare requirements and commitment to code quality\n- Multi-module Maven structure (api, web, webapp, tools, test) separates concerns while maintaining monolithic deployment, balancing flexibility with simplicity\n- Docker and containerization support added as deployment option rather than core architecture, indicating evolution toward modern infrastructure while preserving legacy compatibility",
